1) Research goal
Compare VM, container, Kata Containers, gVisor for interactive DevOps labs by:

Security
Isolation
Performance
Operational suitability
2) Research questions and hypotheses
RQ1: Which runtime gives the strongest practical isolation for untrusted learners?
RQ2: What is the startup/performance cost of stronger isolation?
RQ3: Which runtime gives the best balance for lab UX and infrastructure cost?
Example hypotheses:

H1: VM/Kata provide stronger isolation than plain containers.
H2: Stronger isolation increases startup latency and memory overhead.
H3: gVisor improves syscall isolation with moderate performance penalty.
3) Test matrix
Use fixed combinations:

Dimension	Values
Runtime	VM, container (runc), Kata, gVisor
Workload	Linux CLI lab, Kubernetes lab, Terraform lab
Concurrency	1, 10, 50 simultaneous labs
Trial count	>= 30 per scenario
Control variables:

Same host hardware
Same base image where possible
Same task script and time limit
Same network/storage class
4) Metrics
Security/Isolation
Privilege mode required (root/rootless)
Default capabilities
Syscall filtering/sandbox model
Filesystem and network isolation boundaries
Escape surface review (documented CVEs, attack classes)
Optional controlled breakout tests (safe PoCs only)
Performance
Provisioning latency (click → ready terminal)
Runtime startup time
CPU overhead (% vs baseline task time)
Memory overhead (RSS/working set)
Disk I/O throughput
Network throughput/latency
Operational
Max lab density per node
Failure rate (provisioning/cleanup)
Recovery time after failure
Implementation complexity (LoC/config count/time-to-setup)
5) Experiment procedure
Prepare clean host/cluster.
Warm-up run (not counted).
Execute scenario N times (randomized order).
Collect raw metrics automatically.
Reset environment between runs.
Repeat under different concurrency levels.
Store all logs/artifacts with run IDs.
6) Tooling (suggested)
Load orchestration: Go benchmark runner (your backend can trigger labs)
System metrics: Prometheus + Node Exporter + cAdvisor
Tracing/timing: OpenTelemetry or simple Go timestamps
Network tests: iperf3
Disk tests: fio
CPU/memory tests: sysbench, /proc, cgroup stats
K8s visibility: kubectl top, metrics-server
Security checks: trivy, kube-bench, runtime docs/CVE review
7) Data format (recommended)
Record each run as one row:

run_id, timestamp, runtime, workload, concurrency, startup_ms, ready_ms, cpu_pct, mem_mb, disk_mbps, net_mbps, provision_ok, cleanup_ok, notes
8) Analysis method
Use median + p95 (not only average)
Show boxplots per runtime
Statistical test:
Normal: ANOVA
Non-normal: Kruskal-Wallis + post-hoc Dunn
Report effect size, not only p-values
9) Threats to validity
Host noise / background load
Different maturity of runtime integrations
Non-identical image/kernel paths
Benchmark selection bias (too synthetic)
10) Deliverables
Reproducible scripts
Raw CSV/JSON data
Plots and statistical outputs
Final decision matrix (security vs performance vs ops)
